This is a README for the (Delivery) XML Editor.

Usage:
     script.sh [options]

where
     script.sh = some script file which calls .../java ... model.DEditor 
     options = command line options as defined below:
	-> xsd=filename, where filename is the name of the XML Schema to load
	-> xml=filename, where filename is the name of an XML file to open
	-> cfg=filename, where filename is the name of a customiation / 
		configuration file
	-> -S disables ability to load schema (other than with command line 
	   	xsd=filename)
	-> -X disables ability to load XML file (other than with command line 
		xml=filename)
	-> -N disables ability to make a new file from scratch
	-> -V enables lax checking (faster speed, but not error proof)

Requirements:
	Java version 1.4.0 or higher (you might get it to work with 1.3 plus 
		the JAXP and regex libraries, but I make no guarantees)
	JavaHelp version 1.1 or higher
	Sun Multi Schema XML Validator package (msv) and supporting libraries:
		msv.jar, isorelax.jar, relaxngDatatype.jar, xsdlib.jar
	To be able to get default values and / or run as an applet, you will 
		need my modified version of msv.jar

Customization XML File:
	The top layer should be <config_options>.  Inside that are 
<tree_display> and <edit_dialog>.  Inside each are any number of 
<customization> tags with children <name>, <value>, and <effect>.  
<tree_display> sub elements are used to create RenderInfo classes for 
rendering the tree.  <edit_dialog> sub elements are used to customize the 
dialog produced by choosing "Edit Tag" or double clicking on a node in the 
tree.  Below is a brief explanation of how the tags work.  
 
<effect>'s are displayed when a node in the tree has the attribute inside of a
<customization>'s <name> with the value equalling that in <value>.  For 
instance, for the following customization, 
<customization>
    <name>day</name>
    <value>Wednesday</value>
    <effect>someEffect</effect>
</customization>
when some element is of the form 
<element_name day="Wednesday">...</elementname>
then someEffect is put into place.

tree_display effect meaning: A.B.C.D.E.F
    A = what to display (can be a combination): n = node name, d = display 
	name (include subchildren values if their name is "name"), 
	a = attributes, v = node value
    B = font style: 0 = plain, 1 = bold, 2 = italics, 3 = bold and italic
    C = red value: 0 (no red) to 255 (bright red)
    D = green value: 0 (no green) to 255 (bright green)
    E = blue value: 0 (no blue) to 255 (bright blue)
    F = alpha value: 0 (fully transparent) to 255 (totally opaque)

edit dialog effect meaning
    starts with "Xcm:" ("X- custom message:"): following text displayed with 
	or overlaps existing message depending on X:
	'r' - replace
	'p' - prepend
	'a' - append
	
    "ne": value in edit box is non-editable when effect is on (use "ne+X") to 
	combine "ne" with other options
    starts with "rvm:": replace the value with a message
